CAT = "caautoBody"; SEG = "BOD"
# RSOS 2023 MWA G: Diagnoses and repairs restraint systems, body components, accessories and trim (G-20, G-21)
Q = [
("Before working near an airbag module, what must be done after disconnecting the battery?",
 "Wait the specified time", ["Remove the airbag fuse only", "Crank the engine once", "Turn the ignition to run"],
 "The SRS module has a reserve capacitor that can still fire an airbag. Waiting the maker's specified time lets it discharge."),
("How should a live, undeployed airbag module be carried?",
 "Trim cover facing away from you", ["Trim cover facing your body", "By its wiring connector", "Upside down under one arm"],
 "If it deployed while carried, the bag would expand away from you. It is also set down with the trim cover facing up."),
("Why should a test light never be used on SRS circuits?",
 "It could deploy an airbag", ["It cannot read low voltage", "It drains the SRS capacitor", "It sets a U code only"],
 "A test light or ordinary ohmmeter can pass enough current through an igniter to fire it. Makers specify the tools and resistor simulators to use."),
("What does the clockspring in a steering column do?",
 "Keeps the airbag connected", ["Returns the wheel to centre", "Measures the steering angle", "Locks the steering column"],
 "The clockspring is a coiled ribbon that keeps the driver airbag and wheel switches connected through the full steering range. It must be centred when installed."),
("What happens if a clockspring is installed off centre?",
 "It can tear at full lock", ["The horn sounds constantly", "The airbag deploys at once", "Steering assist is lost"],
 "An off-centre clockspring runs out of ribbon before full lock and tears. That opens the airbag circuit and turns on the SRS lamp."),
("What must be done with seat belt pretensioners after a crash in which they fired?",
 "Replace them", ["Reset them with a scan tool", "Rewind them by hand", "Leave them if the belt works"],
 "Pretensioners use a pyrotechnic or spring charge that works once. Makers also list other parts to replace after a deployment."),
("After seat service, what may the occupant classification system need?",
 "A rezero or calibration", ["A new seat cushion only", "A new airbag module", "A full SRS module flash"],
 "The system weighs the passenger seat to decide whether to enable the passenger airbag. Many makers require it to be zeroed with a scan tool after seat work."),
("Why do disconnected SRS connectors often have shorting bars?",
 "To prevent deployment", ["To keep moisture out", "To test the circuit faster", "To hold the plug in place"],
 "When unplugged, the bar shorts the igniter terminals together so static or stray voltage cannot fire the device."),
("After a windshield replacement, what is often required for driver assistance systems?",
 "Forward camera calibration", ["Rain sensor replacement", "Airbag module reset", "Wheel alignment check only"],
 "Lane keeping and emergency braking rely on a camera behind the glass. A new windshield changes its view slightly, so static or dynamic calibration is needed."),
("Why can a change in thrust angle affect a front radar or camera system?",
 "They aim along the thrust line", ["They read the tire pressures", "They use wheel speed data only", "They draw power from the hubs"],
 "Driver assistance sensors expect to point where the vehicle travels. If the thrust line moves, they may need recalibration after the alignment."),
("A power window reverses on its own when closing. What is a likely cause after a battery change?",
 "Window limits not relearned", ["The window motor is burned out", "The door lock actuator failed", "The window fuse is too small"],
 "Auto-up windows learn their travel limits. After power loss many must be run fully down and up, holding the switch, to relearn."),
("Wipers stop wherever they are when switched off instead of parking. What is likely faulty?",
 "The park switch circuit", ["The washer pump motor", "The worn wiper blades", "The high-speed motor brush"],
 "A park switch inside the motor keeps it running until the arms reach the park position. If that circuit fails, the wipers stop on the glass."),
("How is a water leak into the cabin usually located?",
 "Low-pressure water and a helper", ["High-pressure washer on seals", "Compressed air inside the car", "A vacuum gauge on the doors"],
 "Run water at low pressure over one area at a time, starting low and working up, while a helper inside watches. High pressure can force water past good seals."),
("Which tool helps find a wind noise leak around a door seal?",
 "An ultrasonic leak detector", ["A cooling system tester", "A smoke machine on the engine", "A digital multimeter"],
 "A small ultrasonic transmitter is placed inside and a receiver is moved around the seal outside. The sound passes where the seal leaks."),
("A rear window defogger has one grid line that stays cold. How is the break found?",
 "Voltage readings along the line", ["Resistance with the power on", "A test light on the glass", "Measuring the line's width"],
 "Voltage falls steadily along a working line. Where it suddenly jumps from near feed voltage to near ground is where the line is broken."),
("What is the best way to remove interior trim panels held by clips?",
 "A plastic trim tool", ["A screwdriver blade", "A pair of pliers", "A pry bar"],
 "A wide plastic trim tool spreads the load and does not scratch or crack panels. Clips that break should be replaced so panels do not rattle."),
]
